int arr[10];              // Normal array

int *p = new int[10];     // Dynamic array

3. Why Dynamic Memory Allocation is Used?

Dynamic memory allocation is useful when the required amount of memory is not known at compile time or when memory needs to exist independently of a particular local scope.

Dynamic memory allocation तब useful होती है जब program को कितनी memory चाहिए यह compile time पर निश्चित नहीं होता, या allocated object को उसके local scope से अलग lifetime तक रखना होता है।

Main Uses
Runtime पर memory allocate करना
User की requirement के अनुसार memory लेना
Dynamic arrays बनाना
Dynamic objects बनाना
Linked List, Tree, Graph जैसी data structures बनाना
Large data structures को dynamically manage करना

1. Runtime Memory Allocate
Dynamic allocation allows memory to be allocated during program execution.
Program run होने के दौरान आवश्यकता पड़ने पर memory allocate की जा सकती है।

int *p = new int(50);

2. User Requirement
It allows the program to allocate memory according to the amount of data entered or required by the user at runtime.
User से runtime पर मिलने वाली requirement के अनुसार memory allocate की जा सकती है।

int n;
cin >> n;

int *arr = new int[n];

अगर user 5 देता है:

n = 5
↓
5 integers की memory allocate

3. Dynamic Arrays
Dynamic allocation allows arrays whose size is determined at runtime.
Dynamic allocation से ऐसा array बनाया जा सकता है जिसका size runtime पर तय होता है।

int n;
cin >> n;

int *arr = new int[n];

delete[] arr;

4. Dynamic Objects 
Dynamic allocation can be used to create objects at runtime.
Runtime पर dynamically allocated memory में objects create किए जा सकते हैं।

Student *s = new Student;

5. Linked List, Tree, Graph such as Data Structures 
Dynamic memory allocation is important for dynamic data structures such as linked lists, trees, and graphs.
Linked List, Tree और Graph जैसी dynamic data structures में nodes को runtime पर create करने के लिए dynamic memory allocation बहुत useful है।

Example:
Node *newNode = new Node;

6. Large Data Structures  Dynamically Manage 

It allows large data structures to be allocated according to actual runtime requirements instead of always reserving a fixed amount of storage.

Program की actual requirement के अनुसार बड़ी data structures के लिए memory allocate की जा सकती है, जिससे पहले से fixed amount reserve करने की जरूरत कम होती है।

4. new Operator
The new operator dynamically allocates memory and, for class types, constructs an object in that storage.
new operator runtime पर dynamically memory allocate करता है और class type के लिए उस memory में object को construct करता है।

Syntax
data_type *pointer = new data_type;

Example
int *p = new int;

5. Dynamic Variable Initialization
int *p = new int(50);

Complete example:

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int *p = new int(50);

    cout << "Value = " << *p << endl;

    delete p;

    return 0;
}

6. delete Operator
The delete operator releases the storage of a single object that was dynamically allocated using new.
delete operator new द्वारा dynamically allocated single object की storage को release करता है।

Syntax
delete pointer;

Example
int *p = new int(50);
cout << *p;
delete p;

Important
new → delete

7. Dynamic Array
Dynamic allocation is useful when the required array size is known only at runtime. The program can obtain the size and allocate the required storage dynamically.

जब array का required size compile time पर पता नहीं होता और runtime पर मिलता है, तब dynamic allocation useful होती है।

8. Complete Dynamic Array Program
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int n;

    cout << "Enter array size: ";
    cin >> n;

    int *arr = new int[n];

    cout << "Enter " << n << " values:" << endl;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        cin >> arr[i];
    }

    cout << "Array values:" << endl;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }

    delete[] arr;

    return 0;
}

9. delete[] Operator

delete[] releases the storage of an array allocated using new[].
delete[] उस dynamic array की memory release करता है जिसे new[] से allocate किया गया था।

Correct Pair 

int *arr = new int[5];
delete[] arr;

note->
new       → delete
new[]     → delete[]

10. Dynamic Object
A dynamic object is an object created at runtime using dynamic memory allocation.
Dynamic Object वह object है जो runtime पर dynamically allocated memory में create होता है।

Syntax
ClassName *ptr = new ClassName;

Example
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Student
{
public:
    string name;

    void display()
    {
        cout << "Name: " << name << endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Student *s1 = new Student;

    s1->name = "abc";

    s1->display();

    delete s1;

    return 0;
}
11. Dynamic Object with Constructor

Dynamic Object with Constructor means creating an object dynamically at runtime using the new operator while simultaneously calling its constructor to initialize the object.
Dynamic Object with Constructor का अर्थ है new operator की सहायता से runtime पर object create करना और उसी समय उसके constructor को call करके object को initialize करना।

Syntax
ClassName *pointer = new ClassName(arguments);

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Student
{
public:
    string name;
    int age;

    Student(string n, int a)
    {
        name = n;
        age = a;
    }

    void display()
    {
        cout << "Name: " << name << endl;
        cout << "Age: " << age << endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Student *s1 = new Student("XYZ", 22);

    s1->display();

    delete s1;

    return 0;
}
12. Pointer Role

A pointer is a variable that stores the address of another object or memory location. In dynamic memory allocation, a pointer is used to store the address of dynamically allocated memory and access that memory.
Pointer एक ऐसा variable है जो किसी दूसरे object या memory location का address store करता है। Dynamic memory allocation में pointer dynamically allocated memory का address store करता है और उस memory को access करने में मदद करता है।

int *p = new int(100);
cout << *p;

13. . vs -> Operator
The dot operator (.) is used to access data members and member functions through a normal object.

Student s1;

s1.display();

Pointer to Dynamic Object
The arrow operator (->) is used to access data members and member functions through a pointer to an object.
Student *s1 = new Student;
s1->display();

Equivalent Form
s1->name/(*s1).name

Note-->
Object  → .
Pointer → ->

14. Dynamic Memory Deallocation
Deallocation is the process of releasing dynamically allocated memory when it is no longer required.
Deallocation का मतलब है जब dynamically allocated memory की आवश्यकता समाप्त हो जाए, तब उस memory को release करना।

Example
int *p = new int(10);

// use p

delete p;
p = nullptr;

nullptr assign करना pointer को safely indicate करता है कि अब वह किसी valid object को point नहीं कर रहा।

15. Memory Leak
A memory leak occurs when dynamically allocated memory is no longer needed but is not properly released.
Memory Leak तब होता है जब dynamically allocated memory की जरूरत खत्म हो जाती है लेकिन उसे properly release नहीं किया जाता।

Example
void test()
{
    int *p = new int(100);

    // delete नहीं किया
}

Function समाप्त हो गया, लेकिन allocated memory release नहीं हुई।

Correct
void test()
{
    int *p = new int(100);

    delete p;
}

16. Dangling Pointer

A dangling pointer is a pointer that refers to an object or memory location whose lifetime has ended.
Dangling Pointer वह pointer है जो ऐसी memory/object को point करता है जिसका lifetime समाप्त हो चुका है।

Example
int *p = new int(50);

delete p;
// p is now dangling

//best approach
delete p;
p = nullptr;

17. Double Deletion

Double Deletion occurs when a program attempts to release the same dynamically allocated memory more than once using delete or delete[]. This results in undefined behavior.
Double Deletion तब होता है जब program एक ही dynamically allocated memory को दो या अधिक बार delete या delete[] करने की कोशिश करता है। इससे undefined behavior हो सकता है।

Wrong
int *p = new int(10);

delete p;
delete p;    // Wrong
Better
delete p;
p = nullptr;

18. nullptr

nullptr is the modern C++ null pointer value used to represent a pointer that does not point to an object.
nullptr modern C++ में यह बताने के लिए use होता है कि pointer currently किसी valid object को point नहीं कर रहा।

Example
int *p = new int(10);

delete p;

p = nullptr;

